<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Which medications are most likely to affect hearing?</h3>
<p>Aminoglycoside antibiotics (e.g., gentamicin), platinum‑based chemotherapy (cisplatin, carboplatin), and loop diuretics (furosemide) carry known risks, especially at higher doses or with rapid IV administration. High‑dose aspirin and some NSAIDs can cause reversible tinnitus or temporary hearing changes. Always confirm your specific medication’s risk with your prescriber or pharmacist.</p>

<h3>If I get ringing from a medication, will it go away?</h3>
<p>Often yes with short‑term triggers like high‑dose aspirin or some NSAIDs once the dose is reduced or the medicine is stopped under medical guidance. With certain antibiotics and chemotherapy, changes can be longer‑lasting. Report symptoms quickly and consider a hearing test for objective tracking.</p>

<h3>Should I stop a medication if I notice ear symptoms?</h3>
<p>Do not stop on your own. Many medications are essential for treating serious conditions. Call your prescriber to discuss symptoms and timing. They may adjust your dose, check drug levels, switch therapies, or refer you for hearing monitoring.</p>

<h3>Can I prevent ototoxicity if I must take the medicine?</h3>
<p>You can reduce risk: get a baseline hearing test, follow monitoring plans, avoid loud noise during treatment, keep hydrated if advised, and avoid stacking multiple ototoxic risks without medical guidance. Coordination among your prescriber, pharmacist, and an audiologist is key.</p>


